{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's refutation condition is too weak to guard the asserted contradiction. The claim is that materializing carriage attacks 'can flip a previously accepted node to undecided,' but the refutation test only demands set-identity of the grounded EXTENSION between the closed and original graphs. A materialization can leave the extension set unchanged while altering the attack relation in ways that affect derivation order, status assignments at intermediate stages, or non-extension properties the skeptical semantics relies on. The endpoints (closed graph extension = original graph extension) do not ensure that no label transitions, so the relation is asserted but not protected by its refutation condition.","target":"45a0b912e9598a0f3ae07ae757a81d8623fd75b8760ca96d8ca89a151c2de185"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's refutation condition is mismatched to the asserted contradiction. The claim is that RESIDUE labels promote re-argumentation by naming fixable causes for suspension, making suspension 'actionable.' But the refutation test only checks whether RESIDUE is a pure function of grounded labels and edges (isomorphism invariance). A RESIDUE scheme can be a pure, isomorphic function and still name actionable fixable causes — functionally of structure does not rule out semantic content that triggers re-examination. Thus the refutation condition does not protect the claimed contradiction, leaving the relation between RESIDUE and skeptical semantics unsubstantiated.","target":"9a62db90dfd5bdeabe8e87a532d1c8ac80a79cf8dacc0421bb05bf16b819cc25"}]}